@short_title Multi-tier programming

{0 Eliom PPX syntax extension}

The eliom syntax extension allows to write, in one file, both the
client and server part of your application. The extension is
automatically enabled by the default build system on
[.eliom] and [.eliomi] files.

The extensions presented here are also available with the [eliom.]
prefix.

{1 Sections}

The location of code execution is specified by section annotations. We
can specify that a declaration is performed on the server, or on the
client:

{%wodoc:@ class=server%}
{@ocaml[
  let%server s = ...
]}
{%wodoc:@ class=client%}
{@ocaml[
  let%client c = ...
]}
Or, alternatively:
{%wodoc:@ class=server%}
{@ocaml[
[%%server
  let s = ...
]
]}
{%wodoc:@ class=client%}
{@ocaml[
[%%client
  let c = ...
]
]}

Finally, it's also possible to start a section for the rest of the file:

{@ocaml[
[%%server.start]
let s = ...
]}

In [.eliomi] files, the three forms are available, with signatures:
[val%server x : t], [[%%server: val x : t]], and [[%%server.start]].

Sections are only available at top level and cannot be nested.

Similarly, you can write for example [type%server],  [module%server],
[open%server], etc ...

{2 Shared sections}

Shared sections are also available for code that should be on the
client and on the server.

{%wodoc:@ class=shared%}
{@ocaml[
let%shared sh = ...
]}

This is equivalent to duplicating the code in a client and a server
section. Each copy refers to the declarations of its own side, which
may differ:

{@ocaml[
let%server output s = print_endline s
let%client output s = Dom_html.window##alert (Js.string s)
let%shared () = output "Hello"
]}

Here, [output] is the server function in the server copy, and the
client function in the client copy.

{1 Client fragments}

A client-side expression can be included inside a server section, and
the server can thereafter refer to it: an expression placed inside
[[%client ... ]] will be computed on the client when it receives the
page; but the eventual client-side value of the expression can be
manipulated as a black box on the server.

{%wodoc:@ class=server%}
{@ocaml[
let%server x = [%client 1 + 3 ]
]}

For example, here, the expression [1 + 3] will be evaluated on the
client, but it’s possible to refer server-side to the future value of
this expression (for example, put it in a list). The value of a client
fragment cannot be accessed on the server.

In this case, [x] has type [int Eliom.Client_value.t].
Eliom can't always
infer the type of fragments. In this case, you may annotate the type:
[[%client ( ... : <type> ) ]].

Client fragments cannot be nested.

{2 Shared fragments}

It is also possible to use shared fragments.
{%wodoc:@ class=server%}
{@ocaml[
let%server x = [%shared ...]
]}

It will produce values of type
{!Eliom.Shared.Value.t}.

Shared fragments can contain client fragments.

{1 Injections and escaped values}

Values that have been computed on the server can be used on the client
by prefixing them with a percent symbol [~%]. We call this an
{e injection}.

{@ocaml[
let%server s = 1 + 2
let%client c = ~%s + 1
]}

Here, the expression [1 + 2] is evaluated and bound to variable
[s] on the server.  The resulting value [3] is transferred to
the client together with the Web page, then the expression
[~%s + 1] is computed client-side.  An injection makes it possible to
access client-side a client fragment which has been specified on the
server:

{@ocaml[
let%server x = [%client 1 + 3 ]
let%client c = 3 + ~%x
]}

The value inside the client fragment is extracted by [~%x], whose
value is [4] here.

Any server-side expression can be injected, with parentheses:
[~%(f x)]. The injected expression is evaluated on the server once,
when the server loads the module, just before the declaration that
contains the injection. It can thus only refer to server-side values
defined before this declaration, not to client-side variables. Each
client process receives a copy of the value with its first page: later
changes on the server are not seen by the client. To follow a
server-side value, inject a reactive signal (see
{!val:Eliom.Eliom_react.S.Down.of_react}).

{@ocaml[
let%client server_start = ~%(Unix.gettimeofday ())
]}

Here, [server_start] is the time when the server loaded the module,
not the time of the request.

In a shared section, the server copy uses the value of the injected
expression directly, computed in the same way.

Inside a client fragment, [~%] also accepts the local variables of
the server code around the fragment: their value, when the fragment is
created, is sent with the fragment (see [~%n] in the
{{!section-clientvalues}example below}).

{1 Restrictions}

It is not possible to use injections on values containing a
closure. This includes lazy values, objects, or anything containing
functions. You can use
{!Eliom.Client.server_function} ([let%rpc])
or client or shared fragments to circumvent this limitation.

To extend and customize the serialization from server to client, see
chapter {{!//clientserver/page-wrapping}Wrapping values}.

{1:evaluation Evaluation}

{2 Top-level code}

On the server, top-level declarations are evaluated when the server
loads the module, before any request: no information on a request is
available at that time.

On the client, top-level declarations are evaluated when the client
process starts, before the page is in the DOM. To act on the page, use
{!Eliom.Client.onload}.

{2:clientvalues Global and request client fragments}

Regardless of the construction used and their combination, there is
only one communication from server to client, when the Web page is
sent. Client fragments are not executed when the server meets them:
the server registers them for later execution, and the client
executes them once it has received the page.

A client fragment created when the server loads the module is
{e global}. Global fragments are executed when the client process
starts, together with the client sections, in the order of the
program. This example prints the numbers 1 to 5 in the browser
console, in this order:

{@ocaml[
let%client () = print_endline "1"

let%server f n = ignore [%client (print_endline (string_of_int ~%n) : unit)]

let%server () =
  ignore [%client (print_endline "2" : unit)];
  f 3;
  f 4

let%client () = print_endline "5"
]}

A client fragment created while the server processes a request is a
{e request} fragment. The client executes request fragments when it
receives the response, in the order of their creation on the server.
When the response is a page of an Eliom application (see
{!Eliom.Registration.App}), they are executed once the new content is
in the page, so they can use its elements.

Creating a client fragment in any other context (for example in a
thread that runs after the server has started, outside of any request)
raises an exception.
